nal  space station  we've never played a key role until now  in human exploration  beyond low earth orbit  but with the artemis mission which will  enable us to send humans back to the  moon for the first time esa is a central  part of that esa has built the service  module of the orion crew vehicle  and  the nasa and esa astronauts that will  ride on that capsule to the moon will be  powered by european technology  in 2019 esa ministers committed to its  biggest ever budget and approved an  ambitious portfolio of space programs  while esa cannot compete with the likes  of nasa when it comes to spending esa  provides valuable science services that  have made it an essential partner in  nasa's artemis programme  esa will contribute habitation and  refueling modules along with enhanced  communications  it'll also be responsible for operating  elements on the lunar gateway a  permanent station in orbit around the  moon  the lunar gateway has two functions  really  it's the first place  that humans will go to live and work for  extended periods beyond earth's orbit so  we're really exposing humans to a  location which is in deep space where  the environment and the conditions are  very different from what we find on the  space station this gives us a test bed  somewhere to learn how to live and work  in deep space for long periods of time  it's also somewhere where we can perform  scientific research  which will prepare us for future  exploration in deep space  we're also working on a large lunar  lander which would take cargo logistics  robotics and other equipment to the  surface of the moon as a contribution to  the human missions that are going to  follow during the 2020s  the first  human missions to go return to the  service of the moon are planned for the  latter part of this decade  those could be followed by the build-up  of infrastructure and capability so we  could be seeing a sustained presence on  the moon into the 2020s  sustainability is going to be based on  having the infrastructure  and the facilities to enable long-term  access and if you want to do that on the  moon in a long-term sustainable way  we're going to have to learn to use the  resources that we find locally wherever  we can  if you can take the regolith the ground  up dusty rocky powder that we find at  the lunar surface if we can then bind  that together we can create structures  those structures can protect us from the  very hot and very cold environment so to  create some thermal shielding we can  also use it to create radiation  shielding and so it's regular 3d  printing where you take this stuff and  you 3d print it into the structures you  need is a really exciting technology for  future applications  gravity on the moon is much lower than  it is on earth so launching a rocket off  the lunar surface would potentially be  easier as it requires much less thrust  less thrust means less fuel making room  in the rocket for the essential payloads  to get humans to mars and beyond  the moon's polar regions also have large  supplies of water ice which could be  broken down into oxygen and hydrogen  useful ingredients needed for humans to  breathe and rockets to fly  this return to the moon will prepare us  for mars so we're going to be performing  cutting-edge science the like of which  has never been done before  humans last stepped onto the moon nearly  50 years ago now multiple nations  including china russia and india all  have planned missions  unlike the apollo missions which lasted  just days new missions would be much  longer because we now think the moon can  teach us so much more  off the coast of west africa lanzarote's  volcanic landscape might be the closest  thing to the lunar surface here on earth  it's why issa comes here to run its  pangaea program  a specialized international training  course for astronauts  some of the reasons that we're going to  go back to the moon are to look at very  early planetary system formation so we  can un