onment like you can see  around me so  understanding the places where the most  interesting biology is is very important  uh for training astronauts in terms of  what they should target when they go to  mars and also just for scientists to  understand where we should look for  searching for life on mars  this was a day where they were letting  us be autonomous so we were making some  of the discoveries like we would do on  the lunar surface so some of the things  that we were finding were very  interesting  was an area where there was some  possible maybe hydrothermal vents or  some water interaction coming up and we  could see some alterations in the  minerals so that kind of thing  would be incredibly important and very  valuable if we saw that on the on the  surface of mars this would be an example  of a place that you would stop and do a  lot of sampling for life  the other thing that's really important  is the context of the sample so we need  to know this isn't just a rock that we  plucked off the ground but where is it  is it close to the hot spring is it near  the volcano was it an early sample was  it a later sample all of that kind of  metadata is really critical in  understanding  that geobiology sample in the context of  everything else that was going on in the  environment  the moon could serve as a proving ground  for the technologies of the future  helping humanity learn how to live and  work in space and launch missions to  bodies further than ever before  like mars  if you look at the surface of mars  there's lots of channels that have been  carved by water early on in its its  history it was probably a very different  planet probably a lot more habitable  much more conducive to life potentially  there's a lot we can learn about what  biology is in terms of kind of a feature  of the universe we can learn by going to  mars because perhaps it was habitable  early but maybe life never developed so  we need to understand why that is or if  life develope